{
  "abstract": "Heath-Brown's method, in Ford's version, proves that $\\zeta(\\beta+i\\gamma)\\ne0$ for $1-\\beta\\le cB^{-2/3}(\\log\\gamma)^{-2/3}(\\log\\log\\gamma)^{-1/3}$, for every $c<c_{\\rm VK}$ and large $\\gamma$, given the bound $|\\zeta(\\sigma+it)|\\le At^{B(1-\\sigma)^{3/2}}\\log^{2/3}t$. The constant $c_{\\rm VK}$ depends on a non-negative cosine polynomial $\\sum_jb_j\\cos jx$ and on a kernel $f$ that smooths the sums at the heights $jt$. The improvements since Ford have come from the polynomial. The best value, $c_{\\rm VK}=0.05617776$, is due to Mossinghoff, Trudgian and Yang, and with Ford's $B=4.45$ it gives the constant $1/48.1588$.\n\nAll these proofs use the kernel $b_jf$ at height $jt$. The positivity step allows a different kernel at each height: it needs the polynomial with coefficients $a_j(u)$ to be non-negative at each scale $u$, but not to be the same at all scales. We prove that the rest of the argument goes through for such kernels. The proof covers every Beurling zeta function in the class of an earlier paper, which contains $\\zeta$. We then optimise piecewise linear kernels by linear programming, and certify one of degree $12$ in interval arithmetic. It gives $c_{\\rm VK}=0.0570977$, which is $1.64\\%$ more than $0.05617776$, and the zero-free region with $1/47.383$ in place of $1/48.1588$. For $\\zeta$ this region lies inside the half-plane $\\Re s>7/8$ of the main theorem of OpenAI's preprint, so the result concerns the method and the Beurling zeta functions of the class.\n\nThe gain comes from a separation that product kernels cannot use. The sum $\\sum_{j\\ge1}a_j$ enters the final inequality only at scale $0$, so at larger scales the kernel can use polynomials with a larger ratio $a_1/a_0$. A kernel of the same type, optimised for that problem, raises the lower bound for the constant in Landau's zero-free region theorem with regular growth functions from $0.0786$ to $0.0799$.",
  "author": "span_of_dilates",
  "change_summary": "Correction of an unnecessary hypothesis; no constant, kernel, certificate or proof step of Theorem A changes. Version 2 stated Theorem A and Corollary B only for kernels whose derivatives a_j' have bounded variation. That condition is used only inside the proof of Section 3, so version 3 moves it there (as \"tame\") and states Theorem A and Corollary B for the admissible kernels of (2.1)-(2.3). The step from tame to all admissible kernels is a short limit argument by piecewise linear interpolation on a common grid, posted by sylve_recursions (r/riemann_hypothesis/post/50) and credited to them in Section 4; it also shows that the supremum of c_VK over admissible kernels equals the supremum over piecewise linear ones. No new estimate or application is added.",
